{"externally_confirmed_facts":["`java.security.MessageDigest` is the Java Security API class for computing message digests: fixed-length hashes of arbitrary-sized input. Its documented usage lifecycle is to obtain an instance with `getInstance`, provide input through one or more `update` calls, and complete computation with `digest`; completion resets the digest object. `digest(byte[])` is documented as performing a final update with the supplied input followed by completion.","The reported value `SHA-1` refers to the algorithm argument of a modeled `MessageDigest.getInstance(...)` factory acquisition, rather than bytes passed to `update`, the digest result, or a later digest equality comparison. The supplied detector message says that this algorithm value was `SHA-1` and did not satisfy that detector rule's allow-list.","The current requested CrySL source declares `SPEC java.security.MessageDigest`; it therefore governs that declared receiver/API type, not methods selected solely because they have names such as `getInstance`, `update`, or `digest`. The source does not describe a scanner's concrete type-resolution implementation, so that implementation detail cannot be established from the rule file.","The exact event/matching clause in the current requested CrySL source is:
```
SPEC java.security.MessageDigest

OBJECTS
	java.lang.String algorithm;
	byte preInputByte;
	byte[] preInput;
	int preOffset;
	int preLen;
	java.nio.ByteBuffer preInputByteBuffer;
	byte[] input;
	int offset;
	int len;
	byte[] output;

EVENTS
	g1: getInstance(algorithm);
	g2: getInstance(algorithm, _);
	Get := g1 | g2;

	u1: update(preInputByte);
	u2: update(preInput);
	u3: update(preInput, preOffset, preLen);
	u4: update(preInputByteBuffer);
	Update := u1 | u2 | u3 | u4;
	d1: output = digest();
	d2: output = digest(input);
	d3: digest(output, offset, len);
	DWOU := d2;
	DWU := d1 | d3;
	Digest := DWU | DWOU;
```","Thus, the CrySL source matches these method names and parameter-list forms: `getInstance(algorithm)` and `getInstance(algorithm, _)`; `update(byte)`, `update(byte[])`, `update(byte[], int, int)`, and `update(ByteBuffer)`; and `digest()`, `digest(byte[])`, and `digest(byte[], int, int)`. It does constrain the parameter lists by enumerating those forms and binding the shown variables.","The exact relevant order and constraint clauses in that CrySL source are:
```
ORDER
	Get, (DWOU | (Update+, Digest))+

CONSTRAINTS
	algorithm in {"SHA-256", "SHA-384", "SHA-512"};
	length[preInput] >= preLen + preOffset;
	preOffset >= 0;
	preLen > 0;
	length[output] >= len + offset;
	offset >= 0;
	len > 0;
```
Accordingly, the rule evaluates an allow-list condition on the factory algorithm variable and also evaluates the shown array-bound, nonnegative-offset, and positive-length conditions for the applicable modeled calls.","The current raw CrySL file's algorithm condition contains only `SHA-256`, `SHA-384`, and `SHA-512`. This differs from the reported detector message, which also lists `SHA256`, `SHA384`, and `SHA512`. The supplied sources do not establish which exact CrySL revision was bundled with the reporting detector.","Oracle documents `SHA-1`, `SHA-256`, `SHA-384`, and `SHA-512` as standard `MessageDigest` algorithm names and documents different digest output sizes: SHA-1 is 160 bits (20 bytes), SHA-256 is 256 bits (32 bytes), SHA-384 is 384 bits (48 bytes), and SHA-512 is 512 bits (64 bytes). The standard-name specification says standard names are case-insensitive; it does not list punctuation-free `SHA256`, `SHA384`, or `SHA512` as standard `MessageDigest` names.","Oracle's Java security specifications caution that SHA-1 has cryptanalytic weaknesses and state that an application is responsible for deciding whether an algorithm meets its security requirements. The Java `MessageDigest` API itself does not prescribe a replacement algorithm or a code/data migration procedure.","NIST's hash-function policy recommends transition away from SHA-1 and identifies SHA-2 and SHA-3 as alternatives. Its policy is an algorithm-transition recommendation, not an application-specific migration procedure for stored data or protocols.","The reviewed Java and CrySL sources provide no compatibility guarantee between output values from different digest algorithms and do not define a persisted-digest format, wire-format negotiation mechanism, dual-read process, versioning scheme, or procedure for re-computing historical SHA-1 digests. Because the documented output sizes differ, replacing an unencoded SHA-1 digest with SHA-256, SHA-384, or SHA-512 changes its byte length.","For the Java SE 26 documentation reviewed, SHA-1, SHA-256, and SHA-384 are stated as required `MessageDigest` support; the research source does not establish universal SHA-512 support. `MessageDigest` has existed since Java 1.1, and `update(ByteBuffer)` is documented since Java 1.5. Provider and target-platform documentation remains necessary for a specific runtime's available algorithms.","The Java API documents `MessageDigest.isEqual(byte[], byte[])` as an equality comparison of digest arrays. That API documentation does not classify the surrounding usage as authentication, adversarial integrity, a cache key, a persisted identifier, or a wire-format check."],"sources":[{"relevance":"Official Java API documentation for the purpose, lifecycle, factory behavior, overload semantics, reset behavior, `isEqual`, and API version information for `java.security.MessageDigest`.","limitations":"It does not prescribe a SHA-1 replacement, classify an application's digest purpose, or specify persisted-data or wire-protocol migration procedures.","claim":"Documents MessageDigest creation, update/digest usage, `digest(byte[])` final-update behavior, and digest completion/reset semantics.","authority":"official","url":"https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/26/docs/api/java.base/java/security/MessageDigest.html"},{"relevance":"Official Java SE security specification for standard MessageDigest names, output lengths, algorithm-support statements, and SHA-1 security caution.","limitations":"It does not define application-specific compatibility contracts or migration of existing stored or transmitted digest values.","claim":"Documents SHA-1/SHA-2 standard names and digest lengths and cautions that SHA-1 has cryptanalytic weaknesses.","authority":"official","url":"https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/26/docs/specs/security/standard-names.html"},{"relevance":"Primary source for the requested CrySL rule's declared type, exact event signatures, order expression, and constraints.","limitations":"This is the mutable `master` branch and may not be the exact revision embedded in the detector. It does not explain scanner type resolution, classify digest purpose, or explain the selected three-algorithm policy.","claim":"Declares `SPEC java.security.MessageDigest`, enumerates modeled `getInstance`, `update`, and `digest` call forms, and constrains `algorithm` to `SHA-256`, `SHA-384`, or `SHA-512`.","authority":"primary","url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/CROSSINGTUD/Crypto-API-Rules/master/JavaCryptographicArchitecture/src/MessageDigest.crysl"},{"relevance":"Official NIST hash-function policy relevant to documented SHA-1 transition guidance.","limitations":"It is high-level policy guidance rather than a Java API specification or a migration protocol for application data formats and external contracts.","claim":"Recommends transitioning away from SHA-1 and identifies SHA-2 and SHA-3 as alternatives.","authority":"official","url":"https://csrc.nist.gov/Projects/Hash-Functions/NIST-Policy-on-Hash-Functions"}],"queries_performed":["Reviewed Oracle Java SE 26 `java.security.MessageDigest` API documentation.","Reviewed Oracle Java SE 26 Java Security Standard Algorithm Names specification.","Reviewed the supplied primary CrySL rule URL: `MessageDigest.crysl` in CROSSINGTUD Crypto-API-Rules.","Reviewed NIST's Hash Functions policy page for documented SHA-1 transition guidance."],"externally_unresolved_questions":["The exact CrySL rule revision used by the reporting detector was not supplied. The reported six-name allow-list differs from the current raw `master` rule's three-name allow-list.","The reviewed sources do not establish the digest's application purpose, whether it is an unchanged external contract, whether values are persisted or transmitted, or whether a compatibility migration is applicable.","The reviewed sources do not establish runtime reachability, actual provider selection, target Java runtime/provider support for a particular deployment, or behavior